# counter harness. fun and jac are one potential evaluation when the coordinates match.
# Hessian-vector products are analytic and are not force calls.
# v2 success is under the cap, at the force stop, and Hessian index 0.
# energy and Kabsch RMSD were not applied: the problem file has no energy_tol, rmsd_tol, or per-start reference.
# A trial past the per-atom cap is mapped back inside that ball before the potential runs.
# LJ38 index removes the six rigid modes. A curvature is negative only below -max(force_norm, 1e-8*||H||_F, 1e-6).
# Default row. fun and jac are the harness kernel. A repeated coordinate returns the cached energy and forces and does not add a call. The per-atom cap is 0.2, the same length as the eOn max move. A larger trial is mapped back inside that ball before the potential runs. The map is the identity through 0.18 and smooth beyond it. The SciPy infinity-norm gtol is the problem force norm divided by sqrt(n), so a library stop is inside the Euclidean stop. The callback stops when the Euclidean norm meets the problem value. The iteration ceiling sits above the force-call cap. The harness counter is the budget.
